#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581} :activity/id #uuid "dd6d8fc7-ed4f-5412-9ac7-72ddd6e6fc3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164 :topic/id #uuid "84c65e66-54ce-5cc4-9970-19a6aba25468" :topic/math-academy-id 1888} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124372 :question/id #uuid "c844db9d-a556-5ce4-90e8-2db1b5671579" :question/math-academy-id "e-6684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233408}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "4f190feb-818e-5e2a-8ed1-9a841eb19c11" :step/math-academy-id 24350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125713 :tutorial/content "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function.\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**. So in this case,\n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." :tutorial/id #uuid "cfedbc54-8dd6-532f-9075-109e336b43a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a61d8ec6-2820-5303-a832-f51a73657cc2" :step/math-academy-id 3625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124206 :question/id #uuid "30557b46-f1f0-51dc-94ed-5d84515bc4b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125176}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "ce76583f-0ff9-522f-8324-5c6a6492916a" :step/math-academy-id 3626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124207 :question/id #uuid "c7c94a49-1a4e-51f6-b0e7-213c67f40fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1113"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"} :step/id #uuid "a936946d-a2c6-5c5b-8e3e-ad8903a06ff2" :step/math-academy-id 3629}] :activity/title "Global Extrema of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124693}]}
